diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that super_spine_router_0 is silently black-holing all
traffic between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) — does not exist.
The network is fully healthy and the premise of the ticket is false.

A live ping from pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 completes with 0% packet loss and
round-trip times under 4 ms. A traceroute confirms the exact path: pc_0_0 →
leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_1 → leaf_router_0_1 → pc_0_1 (3 router
hops, TTL decrements from 64 to 61, consistent). super_spine_router_0 is not
on this data path at all. In this DC CLOS design both leaf routers are dual-
homed to both spines, so the spines can route between the leaves directly
without involving the super-spine layer; the super-spine would only be needed
for inter-pod traffic in a larger multi-pod fabric.

super_spine_router_0 itself is also healthy: both eBGP sessions (to
spine_router_0_0 at 172.16.0.1/AS65100 and to spine_router_0_1 at
172.16.0.3/AS65101) are Established, and both peers are advertising two
prefixes each. The super-spine's RIB shows both 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24
as valid with paths from both spines; one spine is preferred over the other
solely by the standard eBGP best-path tie-breaker (router-ID comparison),
which is normal operation, not a fault.

Root cause: there is no fault — the ticket describes a non-existent problem.
No corrective action is required.
